== 5. Other Things to Apply Yourselves To

And just as you#super[+] must beware of these five things, so there are various other things you#super[+] must carefully and conscientiously apply yourselves#super[+] to, if ever you#super[+] would be found faithful and constant in this great duty of closet prayer.

Now they are these:

=== a. Lament Greatly and Mourn Bitterly over the Neglect of This Duty

First, lament greatly and mourn bitterly over the neglect of this choice duty. He who does not make it a matter of conscience to mourn over the neglect of this duty will never make it a matter of conscience to perform this duty. Oh, that your#super[+] heads were waters, and your#super[+] eyes a fountain of tears, that you#super[+] might weep day and night for the great neglect of closet prayer (Jer 9:1)! He who mourns most for the neglect of this duty will be found most in the practice of this duty. He who most makes it a matter of conscience to accuse, arraign, and condemn himself for neglecting closet prayer will most make it a matter of conscience to give himself up to closet prayer. It is said of Adam that he turned his face toward the garden of Eden and from his heart bitterly lamented his great fall. Oh, that you#super[+] would turn your#super[+] faces toward your#super[+] closets and bitterly lament how seldom you#super[+] go into them!

=== b. Make Private Prayer Your Constant Trade

Secondly, habituate yourselves#super[+], accustom yourselves#super[+], to closet prayer. Make private prayer your#super[+] constant trade. Frequency breeds familiarity, and familiarity confidence. We can go freely and boldly into the house of a friend whom we often visit. What we are accustomed to, we do with ease and delight. A man who is habituated or accustomed to writing, reading, riding, running, or playing this or that musical instrument does it all with delight and ease. And so a man who habituates himself to closet prayer will carry it on with delight and ease.

=== c. Keep a Diary of All Your Closet Experiences

Thirdly, keep a diary of all your#super[+] closet experiences (Deut 7:18–19; Ps 66:12). Oh, carefully record and write down all your#super[+] closet blessings! Oh, be often reading over your#super[+] closet experiences, and be often meditating and pondering on your#super[+] closet experiences! There is no way like this to inflame your#super[+] love for closet prayer and to bind your#super[+] hearts to this secret trade of private prayer.

Oh, remember that at one time you#super[+] went into your#super[+] closets with hard hearts and dry eyes, but before you#super[+] came out of your#super[+] closets, ah, how sweetly, how evangelically, how powerfully you#super[+] were melted and humbled before the Lord (Ps 6:6; 39:12; 56:8)! Oh, remember how at another time you#super[+] went into your#super[+] closets clouded and in darkness, but came out of your#super[+] closets with as glorious a shining of God on your#super[+] souls as Moses had on his face when he came down from the mountain from speaking with God (Exod 34:28–29)! Oh, remember how often you#super[+] have gone into your#super[+] closets with cold, frozen spirits, but before you#super[+] came out of your#super[+] closets, what a fire God has kindled in your#super[+] souls, what a spirit of burning you#super[+] have found in your#super[+] hearts (Luke 24:31–32; Isa 4:4)! Oh, remember how often you#super[+] have gone into your#super[+] closets cramped and tongue-tied, but before you#super[+] came out, how your#super[+] souls have been like the chariots of Amminadib (Song 6:12)! Oh, remember what power God has given you#super[+] against corruptions in your#super[+] closets, and what strength God has given you#super[+] against temptations in your#super[+] closets! Oh, remember the sweet revelations of divine love that you#super[+] have had while in your#super[+] closets! Oh, remember the secret visits, the secret kisses, the secret embraces, the secret whispers, the secret love-tokens that Christ has given you#super[+] in your#super[+] closets! Oh, seriously ponder these things, and then closet duties will be sweet to you#super[+]!

It was a sweet saying of Bernard: “O saint, do you not know that your husband Christ is bashful and will not be intimate in company? Withdraw by meditation into your closet, or into the fields, and there you will have Christ’s embraces.” #emph[Meditatio nutrix orationis], “Meditation is the nurse of prayer.” Oh, the more any man meditates on his closet experiences, the more he will find his heart bound to closet duties. The more you#super[+] ponder closet experiences, the sweeter closet experiences will be to your#super[+] souls. And the sweeter closet experiences are to your#super[+] souls, the more your#super[+] souls will delight to be with God in your#super[+] closets.

Pliny tells us of one Messala Corvinus, whose memory was so bad that he forgot his own name. And I am afraid that many of your#super[+] memories are so bad that you#super[+] forget your#super[+] closet blessings, your#super[+] closet experiences.

I have read of a plague once at Athens of such a kind that it took away the memories of those who were infected with it, so that they forgot their own names. Oh, that I had no cause to fear that some plague or other has so taken away the memories of many that they have quite forgotten their closet experiences! Well, friends, remember this: though stony hearts are bad, yet iron memories are good. And oh, that you#super[+] would all labor for iron memories, so that you#super[+] may remember and ponder your#super[+] closet experiences! I have read of the heathen, how they made use of white and black stones for these two ends. First, they gave them to persons at their trial before the judges. If any were condemned to death, they gave him a black stone, but if acquitted and set free, a white stone. The Holy Spirit seems to allude to this custom in Revelation 2:17: “To the one who overcomes, I will give … a white stone.” A second use of those stones was this: that by them they might keep an account of all the good days or evil days they had met with in their lives. So Giacopo Senzaro, having been long in love and much thwarted about his marriage, filled a pot full of black stones, putting only one white stone among them; and being asked the reason, he answered, “There will come one white day,” meaning his wedding day, “which will make amends for all my black days.”

Ah, friends! How often God has given you#super[+] the white stone in your#super[+] closets! Certainly, you#super[+] have had more white stones than black stones. Your#super[+] closet blessings and experiences have outnumbered your#super[+] public trials and miseries. O sirs! If you#super[+] only counted your#super[+] good days by the white stones you#super[+] have had in your#super[+] closets, it would make you#super[+] more in love with closet prayer than ever.

=== d. Do Not Spend So Much Time in Public Duties That None Is Left for Private

Fourthly, be sure that you#super[+] do not spend so much of your#super[+] precious time in public duties and ordinances that you#super[+] can spare none for private duties, for secret services. Though Pharaoh’s cows ate one another up, our duties must not eat one another up (Gen 41:4). Public duties must not eat up family duties; family duties must not eat up public duties; neither of them must eat up closet duties. The wisdom of a Christian sparkles and shines most brightly in giving every duty its proper time and place. I was going to say that he is either no Christian, or at least no excellent Christian, who is all eye to read, or all ear to hear, or all tongue to speak, or all knee to bow, to kneel, to pray (Eccl 8:5). Ah, how many there are who spend so much time hearing this man and that, and running here and there from meeting to meeting, that they have no time to meet with God in their closets! O sirs! Your#super[+] duties are never so lovely and pleasing, they are never so radiant and beautiful, as when they are performed in their proper season and in their proper order.

Oh, how wise the men of this world are in ordering all their worldly affairs so that no one business interferes with another! They set apart a suitable portion of time for each business. They allot an hour for one business, two for another, three for another, etc. Oh, that we were as wise for our souls, as wise for eternity, as they are for this world! Oh, that our hearts would so take counsel with our heads that we might never lack a fit time to seek God in a corner! That devil who loves to set one man against another, and one nation against another, and one Christian against another, that devil loves to set one ordinance against another, and one duty against another. That is why, on the one hand, he stirs up some to exalt public prayers in opposition to secret prayer. And on the other hand, he stirs up others to exalt private duties in opposition to all public duties. Yet all Christians are bound by God to manage one sort of duty so as not to shut out another sort. Every Christian must find time and room for every duty that is incumbent on him.

=== e. Love Christ with a More Inflamed Love

Fifthly, love Christ with a more inflamed love. Oh, strengthen your#super[+] love for Christ, and your#super[+] love for closet duties! Lovers dearly love to be alone, to be in a corner together (Song 7:10–12). Certainly, the more any man loves the Lord Jesus, the more he will delight to be with Christ in a corner. There was a great deal of love between Jonathan and David (1 Sam 18–20); and their private fellowship, their secret communion with one another, matched their love. They were always at their best when in the field together, or when in a corner together, or when behind the door together, or when locked up together. And it would be just the same with you#super[+] if you#super[+] only loved the Lord Jesus Christ with a higher and a more inflamed love. You#super[+] would always be at your#super[+] best when you#super[+] were most with Christ in a corner.

Divine love is like a rod of myrtle, which, as Pliny reports, makes the traveler who carries it in his hand so lively and cheerful that he never faints or grows weary. Ah, friends, if you#super[+] only loved the Lord Jesus with a stronger, with a higher love, you#super[+] would never faint in closet duties, nor would you#super[+] ever grow weary of closet duties. Just as the Israelites moved their tents from Mithcah to Hashmonah, from sweetness to swiftness, as the names mean (Num 33:29), so the sweetness of divine love will make a man move swiftly on in the way of closet duties. Divine love will make all closet duties easier to the soul, and more pleasant and delightful to the soul. And therefore, do all you#super[+] can to strengthen your#super[+] love for Christ and your#super[+] love for closet work.

It was observed among the early Christians that they were so full of love for one another that they could become as well acquainted with one another in half an hour as in half a year. O sirs, if only your#super[+] hearts were fuller of love for Christ and for closet duties, you#super[+] would quickly be better acquainted with them; you#super[+] would quickly know what secret communion with Christ behind the door means.

=== f. Be Fixedly Resolved to Keep Close to Closet Duties

Sixthly, be highly, thoroughly, and fixedly resolved, in the strength of Christ, to keep close to closet duties in the face of all the difficulties and discouragements that you#super[+] may meet with (Ps 44:17–20). A man of no resolution, or of weak resolution, will be won with a nut and lost with an apple. Satan, and the world, and carnal relatives, and your#super[+] own hearts will throw in many things to discourage you#super[+] and draw you#super[+] away from closet prayer. But be nobly and firmly resolved to keep close to your#super[+] closets, let the world, the flesh, and the devil do and say what they can. Daniel was a man of invincible resolution. Rather than omit praying in his room, he would be cast into the den of lions. Of all the duties of religion, Satan is the deadliest enemy to this one of secret prayer: partly because secret prayer foils him in his most secret designs, plots, and schemes against the soul; and partly because secret prayer is so musical and delightful to God; and partly because secret prayer is of such rare use and advantage to the soul; and partly because it does not lay the soul so open to pride, vainglory, and worldly applause as prayer in the synagogue does. And therefore, he would rather have a man pray a thousand times in the synagogues, on the street corners, or behind a pillar, than pray once in his closet. And therefore, you#super[+] need to steel your#super[+] hearts with holy courage and resolution, so that whatever suggestions, temptations, oppositions, or objections you#super[+] may encounter, you#super[+] will yet keep close to closet prayer.

There is no better bulwark in the day of battle than a heroic resolution of heart before the day of battle. Sanctified resolutions greatly weaken and discourage Satan in his assaults. They greatly daunt and dishearten him in all his attempts against the soul. The man who is not steadfastly resolved to seek the Lord in a corner, even if all the powers of darkness rise up against him, will never be at peace in his closet for long. O sirs! Divine fortitude, holy resolutions, will make you#super[+] like a wall of brass that no arrows can pierce. They will make you#super[+] like tested armor that no shot can hurt. They will make you#super[+] like that angel who rolled away the stone from the door of the tomb (Matt 28:2). They will either enable you#super[+] to remove the greatest mountains of opposition that lie between you#super[+] and closet prayer, or else they will enable you#super[+] to step over them.

Luther was a man of great resolution and a man who spent much time in closet prayer. Nehemiah was another such man. He met with so much opposition that, had he not been steeled by a strong and unyielding resolution, he could never have rebuilt the temple, but would have sunk in the middle of his work. And he was a man given to private prayer, as I have shown at the beginning of this treatise. Who was more resolute than David? And who was more given to secret prayer than David? I might say the same of Paul, Basil, and many others who have been famous in their generations.

O sirs! Sanctified resolutions for closet prayer will chain you#super[+] faster to closet prayer than Ulysses’ resolutions ever chained him to the mast of the ship. It was a noble resolution that kept Ruth close to her mother-in-law when her sister-in-law Orpah only paid her courtesies, kissed her, and took her leave of her (Ruth 1:10–20). Just be nobly resolved for closet prayer, and then you#super[+] will keep close to it when others only court it and take their leave of it.

In the Salentine country, mention is made of a lake that is always brimful. However much you put in, it never runs over; however much you draw out, it is always full. The resolution of every Christian for closet prayer should be like this lake, always brimful. Come life, come death, come honor or reproach, come loss or gain, come liberty or bonds, come what may, the true-bred Christian must be fully and constantly resolved to keep close to his closet.

=== g. Labor for a Greater Effusion of the Holy Spirit

Seventhly, labor for a greater effusion of the Holy Spirit; for the greater measure any man has of the Spirit of God, the more that man will delight to be with God in secret. “I will pour out on the house of David and on the people of Jerusalem a spirit of grace and supplication … The land will mourn, each clan apart: the clan of the house of David apart, and their wives apart; the clan of the house of Nathan apart, and their wives apart; the clan of the house of Levi apart, and their wives apart; the clan of Shimei apart, and their wives apart; and all the remaining clans, each clan apart, and their wives apart” (Zech 12:10, 12–14). Note that in the last of the last days, when men shall generally be under a greater effusion of the Holy Spirit than ever (Joel 2:28–29; Isa 44:3), then they shall be more given up to secret prayer than ever. There will never be such praying apart, and such mourning apart, as there will be when the Lord pours out His Spirit most richly, gloriously, abundantly, on His poor people. Then everyone shall pour out his tears and his soul before God in a corner, to show the soundness of their sorrow and to show their sincerity by their secrecy, for #emph[Ille dolet vere, qui sine teste dolet], “He grieves in earnest who grieves without a witness.”

Certainly, the more any man is now under the blessed pouring out of the Spirit of Christ, the more that man gives himself up to secret communion with Christ. Every man is with Christ in his closet more or less, just as he is more or less under the anointings of the Spirit of Christ. The more any man has of the Spirit of Christ, the more he loves Christ; and the more any man loves Christ, the more he delights to be with Christ alone. Lovers love to be alone. The more any man has of the Spirit of Christ, the more his heart will be set on pleasing Christ. Now, nothing pleases Christ more than the secret prayers of His people. “O my dove, in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs, let me see your face, let me hear your voice; for your voice is sweet, and your countenance is lovely” (Song 2:14). And therefore such a man will spend much time in secret prayer. The more any man has of the Spirit of Christ, the more his heart will be set on glorifying and exalting Christ. Now, nothing glorifies Christ more, or exalts Him more, than secret prayer; and therefore, the more any man has of the Spirit of Christ, the more that man will be found in secret prayer.

There are many persons who say they would be more in their closets than they are, but that they meet with many hindrances, many engagements, many distractions, many temptations, many oppositions, many difficulties, many discouragements which prevent them. Ah, friends! If you#super[+] had a greater measure of the Holy Spirit upon you#super[+], none of these things would ever be able to hinder your#super[+] secret trade heavenward. If you#super[+] had a richer anointing of the Spirit upon you#super[+], you#super[+] would never plead, “A lion is in the road! A fierce lion roams the public square!” (Prov 26:13). But if there were a thousand lions between you#super[+] and your#super[+] closets, you#super[+] would either step over them or make your#super[+] way through them, so that you#super[+] might enjoy communion with Christ in your#super[+] closets.

=== h. Be Frequent in the Serious Consideration of Eternity

Eighthly and lastly, if ever you#super[+] would keep close to private prayer, be frequent in the serious consideration of eternity. Oh, see eternity standing at the end of every closet prayer, and this will make you#super[+] pray effectually in your#super[+] closets.

O sirs! Every work you#super[+] do is a step toward a blessed or a cursed eternity. Every movement, every action in this life, is a step toward eternity. As every step that a traveler takes brings him forward to his journey’s end, so every step that a man takes in the secret ways of righteousness and holiness, such as closet duties are, brings him nearer to his journey’s end. It brings him nearer to a blessed eternity. Just as every step the sinner takes in a way of wickedness brings him nearer to hell, so every step that a saint takes in a way of holiness brings him nearer to heaven. Just as every step that a wicked man takes in the ways of unrighteousness brings him nearer to a cursed eternity, so every step that a godly man takes in a way of righteousness brings him nearer to a blessed eternity.

Zeuxis, the famous painter, was so exceedingly careful and cautious in drawing all his lines that he would let no piece of his go out into the world to be seen by men until he had turned it over and over, and viewed it from this side and that, again and again, to see if he could spot any fault in it. When he was asked why he was so painstaking and so long in drawing his lines, he answered, #emph[Aeternitati pingo], “I paint for eternity.” O sirs! We all pray for eternity, we fast for eternity, we read for eternity, we hear for eternity, we wait for eternity, we weep for eternity. And therefore, oh, how painstaking, how exact, how wise, how faithful, how careful, how diligent, how unwearied we should be in all our closet duties and services, since all we do is with a view to eternity! Friends! You#super[+] must all before long be eternally blessed or eternally cursed; eternally happy or eternally miserable; eternally saved or eternally damned; eternally accepted or eternally rejected. And therefore, what infinite cause you#super[+] have to shut your#super[+] closet doors often and to plead mightily with God in a corner for the lives of your#super[+] poor, precious, and immortal souls, that they may be eternally saved in the Great Day of our Lord Jesus! O sirs! When any hindrances to closet prayer present themselves to you#super[+], seriously remember eternity, and that will remove them.

It is related of one Pachomius that whenever he felt any unlawful desires arise in his mind, he used to drive them away with the remembrance of eternity.

The same author tells a story from #emph[Benedictus Rhexanus] of an ungodly fellow who could not sleep one night: as he seriously considered death and eternity and the damned lying in hell, he could not rest, but eternity kept running through his mind. He would gladly have shaken off these thoughts, as gnawing worms. So he followed sports, pastimes, and merrymaking, and sought out companions like himself, and often sat so long over his drink that he put his conscience to sleep, and so seemed to get some rest. But when he woke, his conscience flew in his face and kept putting sad thoughts of eternity into his mind. Of all things in the world, he could not bear to be kept awake at night. But it so happened that, being sick, he was kept awake one night and could not sleep at all, and these thoughts arose in him: “What? Is it so tedious, then, to be kept from sleep one night and to lie a few hours in the dark? Oh, what is it, then, to be kept in torments and everlasting darkness! I am here in my own house on a soft bed in the dark, kept from sleep for only one night. But to lie in flames and endless misery, how dreadful that must be!” These and similar meditations were the happy means of this young man’s conversion.

I have read a notable story of one Theodorus, a young Christian man in Egypt, who, when there was a great deal of feasting, mirth, and music in his father’s house, withdrew from all the company; and when he was alone, he thought to himself, “Here is contentment and delight enough for the flesh. I may have what I desire, but how long will this last? This will not hold out long.” Then, falling down on his knees before the Lord in secret, he said, “Lord, my heart is open to You. I truly do not know what to ask except only this: Lord, do not let me die eternally. O Lord, You know I love You. Let me live eternally to praise You.”

If there is any way or means on earth to bring us to our knees before God in secret, it is serious and solemn thoughts of eternity. Oh, that the fear of eternity might fall on all your#super[+] souls! Oh, that you#super[+] would all seriously consider that after a short time has passed you#super[+] must all enter an eternal state! Oh, consider that eternity is an infinite, endless, bottomless gulf which no line can fathom, no time can reach, no age can extend to, no tongue can express. It is a duration always present, a being always in being. It is #emph[unum perpetuum hodie], “one perpetual day,” which shall never see light. O sirs! This is, and must be, for a lamentation: that eternity is a thing most men never think of, or else think of very lightly—a snatch and away, as dogs are said to lap and run at the river Nile. But if ever you#super[+] would have your#super[+] hearts chained to your#super[+] closets and to closet duties—as the men of Tyre chained their god Apollo to a post, so that they might be sure of him—then seriously and frequently ponder eternity, and with those forty valiant martyrs be always crying out, ὦ ἀϊδιότης, ὦ ἀϊδιότης, “O eternity, eternity!”

Mr. Wood, after some holy conversation, fell to musing and cried out before all present for nearly half a quarter of an hour on end, “Forever, forever, forever.” Augustine’s prayer was, “Hack me, hew me, burn me here, but spare me hereafter, spare me hereafter.” Certainly, if Christians would only spend one quarter of an hour every day in solemn thoughts of eternity, it would make them more in love with closet prayer than ever. Indeed, it would make them more fearful of omitting closet prayer than ever, and more careful and conscientious in carrying out all closet duties than ever.

And so, according to my weak measure, I have given out all that the Lord has at present graciously given in to my poor soul concerning this most necessary, most glorious, and most useful point of points: closet prayer. By assisting grace, I will follow this poor piece with my prayers, that it may be so blessed from on high as to work mightily for the internal and eternal welfare of reader, hearer, and writer alike.
